Article 3 The following adjudicative documents rendered by the People's Court shall be published on the Internet:
(1) Criminal, civil, and administrative judgments;
(2) Criminal, civil, administrative, and enforcement rulings;
(3) Orders of payment;
(4) Notices of rejection of a petition for retrial in criminal, civil, administrative, and enforcement cases;
(5) Decisions on state compensation;
(6) Decisions on compulsory medical treatment or decisions rejecting an application for compulsory medical treatment;
(7) Decisions on execution and modification of criminal punishments;
(8) Decisions on detention or fine for acts obstructing litigation or enforcement, decisions on early release from detention, and decisions on review made in response to applications for reconsideration against sanctions such as detention or fines;
(9) Administrative mediation agreements and mediation agreements in civil public interest litigation;
(10) Other adjudicative documents that have the effect of suspending or terminating litigation proceedings, or that affect the substantive rights and interests of the parties, or have a significant impact on the procedural rights and interests of the parties.

Article 4 Adjudicative documents rendered by the People's Court shall not be published on the Internet in any of the following circumstances:
(1) Those involving state secrets;
(2) Those concerning juvenile delinquency;
(3) Those concluded by mediation or confirming the validity of a people's mediation agreement, except where publication is truly necessary for the protection of national interests, public interests, or the lawful rights and interests of others;
(4) Divorce proceedings or matters involving the custody or guardianship of minor children;
(5) Other circumstances where the People's Court considers publication on the Internet inappropriate.

Article 8 When publishing adjudicative documents on the Internet, the People's Court shall carry out anonymization of the names of the following persons:
(1) The parties and their legal representatives in cases of marital and family disputes or succession disputes;
(2) The victims in criminal cases and their legal representatives, plaintiffs in incidental civil actions and their legal representatives, witnesses, and experts;
(3) Minors and their legal representatives.

Article 9 When carrying out anonymization in accordance with Article 8 of these Provisions, the following methods shall be applied:
(1) The surname shall be retained, with the given name replaced by a '某' (Mou);
(2) For ethnic minority names, the first character shall be retained, with the remaining content replaced by '某';
(3) For the Chinese translation of names of foreigners or stateless persons, the first character shall be retained, with the remaining content replaced by '某'; for the English names of foreigners or stateless persons, the first English letter shall be retained, and the other content shall be deleted.
Where duplication occurs after anonymization of different names, differentiation shall be made by adding Arabic numerals after the name.

Article 10 When publishing adjudicative documents on the Internet, the People's Court shall delete the following information:
(1) Personal information of natural persons such as home address, contact details, identity card number, bank account number, health status, license plate number, certificate number of title to chattels or immovable property;
(2) Information of legal persons and other organizations such as bank account number, license plate number, certificate number of title to chattels or immovable property;
(3) Information involving trade secrets;
(4) Information involving personal privacy in disputes such as family affairs and personality rights;
(5) Information involving technical investigation measures;
(6) Other information that the People's Court considers inappropriate for disclosure.
Where deletion of information in accordance with the first paragraph of this Article affects the correct understanding of the adjudicative document, the symbol '×' shall be used for partial substitution.

Article 11 When publishing adjudicative documents on the Internet, the People's Court shall retain the following information of the parties, legal representatives, authorized agents, and defenders:
(1) Except for those that have been anonymized in accordance with Article 8 of these Provisions, where the party and its legal representative are natural persons, their names, dates of birth, gender, and the county/district to which their domicile belongs shall be retained; where the party and its legal representative are legal persons or other organizations, their names, domiciles, organization codes, and the names and titles of the legal representatives or principal responsible persons shall be retained;
(2) Where the authorized agent or defender is a lawyer or a basic-level legal service worker, his/her name, practice certificate number, and the name of the law firm or basic-level legal service institution shall be retained; where the authorized agent or defender is another person, his/her name, date of birth, gender, the county/district to which his/her domicile belongs, and his/her relationship with the party shall be retained.

Article 13 The Supreme People's Court shall supervise and guide the work of courts nationwide in publishing adjudicative documents on the Internet. The Higher People's Courts and Intermediate People's Courts shall supervise and guide the work of courts within their respective jurisdictions in publishing adjudicative documents on the Internet.
The trial management office of each People's Court, or another institution that undertakes trial management functions, shall be responsible for the management of the court's publication of adjudicative documents on the Internet and shall perform the following duties:
(1) Organizing and guiding the publication of adjudicative documents on the Internet;
(2) Supervising and assessing the work of publishing adjudicative documents on the Internet;
(3) Coordinating the handling of complaints and opinions from the public regarding the disclosure of adjudicative documents;
(4) Coordinating with technical departments to provide technical support and guarantees;
(5) Other related management work.
